WEBVTT

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:08.476
In the 256 by 256 picture, about a quarter of the places need the full 1000

00:00:08.476 --> 00:00:09.544
repetitions.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:13.517
What share of all the arithmetic in the picture do those places take?

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:15.555
Pause and write a percentage.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:37.102
Each place (i, j) becomes a middleman pair through two lines: cx = j/256 × 2.5 − 2.0

00:00:37.102 --> 00:00:41.566
and cy = i/256 × 2.5 − 1.25.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.531
Now the arithmetic itself.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:49.309
The code keeps a second, moving pair of numbers, begins it at (0, 0), and repeats a

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:54.672
handful of lines on it: square it in a particular way, add the middleman pair, and

00:00:54.672 --> 00:00:56.765
check how far it is from (0, 0).

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.826
When it gets farther than 2, the loop is left, and the number of repetitions done is

00:01:01.826 --> 00:01:02.219
stored.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:03.999
I objected twice.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.440
First: if the moving pair begins at zero, and squaring zero gives zero, how can

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.793
anything ever change?

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.066
Second: the explanations used a word for what the point does, and a point does not

00:01:16.066 --> 00:01:16.367
move.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.755
Both questions are settled only by executing the code by hand, with real numbers.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:26.572
Keep one question in mind while you calculate: could you have predicted any of these

00:01:26.572 --> 00:01:28.116
counts before doing the work?

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:41.115
In English: x2, y2 and w begin at 0, and the counter iters at 0.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:48.013
Before each repetition, test two things: is x2 + y2 at most 4, and is iters below

00:01:48.013 --> 00:01:50.036
max_iters, which is 1000?

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.507
If both are true, do lines A to F once and add 1 to iters.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:59.873
If either is false, leave the loop, and store iters, the number of repetitions done.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.740
Line A: subtract y2 from x2 and add cx.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.485
Line B: from w subtract x2, then y2, then add cy.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:15.998
Line C squares x, line D squares y, line E adds x and y into z, and line F squares z

00:02:15.998 --> 00:02:16.595
into w.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.617
x = cx and y = cy.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:30.834
Beginning at 0 does change things: the first repetition lands exactly on the

00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:31.794
middleman pair.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:32.908
Pause.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.116
Fill the table to 6 decimals.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:56.785
The x column is 0.730564, 1.023958, 1.538725, 2.857909, and the x2 column is

00:02:56.785 --> 00:03:04.649
0.533724, 1.048491, 2.367675, 8.167644.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:11.720
Before repetition 6 the check is 8.167644, above 4, so the loop is left.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:17.446
The count is iters = 5, stored at out[33023].

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.568
Pause.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.883
Write every subtraction on paper.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:41.118
After repetition 1: x2 = 1.890625, y2 = 0.390625, z = −2.0, w = 4.0.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.758
Before repetition 2 the check is 2.281250.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:04:06.739
Then x = 1.890625 − 0.390625 − 1.375 = 0.125, and y = 4.0 − 1.890625 − 0.390625 −

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:10.814
0.625 = 1.09375.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.491
Before repetition 3 the check is 1.211914.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:24.373
Then x = −2.555664 and y = −0.351562.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:33.222
Before repetition 4 the check is 6.655015, above 4, so the loop is left with iters =

00:04:33.222 --> 00:04:33.424
3.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:43.657
Copy the three checks in order: 2.281250, 1.211914, 6.655015.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.856
The check went down before it went up.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:51.351
Getting bigger every repetition is not guaranteed; only "above 4" is final.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:57.844
Line C makes x2 = x × x, and line D makes y2 = y × y.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.443
So the check is the square of the distance from (0, 0).

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:08.025
Distance above 2 means check above 4, because 2 × 2 = 4, and the comparison needs no

00:05:08.025 --> 00:05:08.915
square root.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:15.063
The square root of 6.655015 is 2.5797.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:36.439
At 256, x2 = 3.731236 and y2 = 1.350498, so the check is 5.081734 and iters = 1.

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:40.886
At 32, the same table as before gives iters = 3.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:47.502
It is the same (9, 7) on the image paper with two different counts: iters belongs to

00:05:47.502 --> 00:05:49.443
(cx, cy), never to (i, j).

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:52.358
Pause.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:53.798
Fill everything.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.499
The average is 248.3 repetitions.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:11.423
Places at 1000 are 24.3 percent of the places but do 97.7 percent of the work.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:20.959
The four bands carry 2.8, 46.4, 47.6 and 3.1 percent of the work, so the speedup is

00:06:20.959 --> 00:06:22.287
2.10, not 4.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:30.211
Collect: 5, 3, a check that went down then up, 1 and 3 for the same (9, 7), 97.7

00:06:30.211 --> 00:06:31.536
percent, 2.10.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:32.658
Pause.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.877
Write the one fact they all point to.

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:45.113
The cost of a place ranges from 1 to 1000 repetitions.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:50.877
It cannot be read off (i, j) or (cx, cy) before the loop is done; even the check

00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:52.841
value does not move steadily.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.677
And the costly places sit together in the middle of the picture.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:01.948
Any plan that splits by count of places, not by work, is wrong before it starts.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:08.224
The tempting answer, "each place is one task, so equal numbers of places are equal

00:07:08.224 --> 00:07:10.897
work", measures at 2.10 instead of 4.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:17.597
This picture is used in the course for one reason: its work is uneven and

00:07:17.597 --> 00:07:18.469
unpredictable.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:20.719
Most real workloads are the same.

00:07:21.070 --> 00:07:25.786
Some customer requests are cheap and some are expensive, and nobody knows which

00:07:25.786 --> 00:07:26.979
until they are done.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:31.661
Splitting work into equal piles of items does not split the effort equally.

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:35.253
Any speed trick for this picture has to deal with that fact.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:41.539
The answer is 97.7 percent of all the arithmetic.

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:42.935
Mark your tally.

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:52.551
You must share the picture among 4 workers without knowing any iters in advance.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:55.076
Bands of i gave 2.10.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:57.926
What would you use instead, and what do you expect?

00:07:58.270 --> 00:07:59.038
Pause.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:11.854
Interleave: worker k takes every 4th value of i, so k, k + 4, k + 8, and so on.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:16.381
Each worker then gets a slice of every band, cheap and costly alike.

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:26.879
On the real iters the shares are 25.2, 25.0, 24.9 and 25.0 percent: a speedup of

00:08:26.879 --> 00:08:28.655
3.97 out of 4.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:33.831
That needs no knowledge of iters, only the fact that costly places sit together.

